#
# Wave 4 — the offline repair A/B (scripts/audit_repair.py). Recorded
# 2026-08-21 on main @ 924eb22, verify.py blob bbdcfddc2d11, index and
# registry hashes unchanged from Wave 3.
#
#   release_release-2.jsonl   the RAW answer set: one --release run at
#       --production-planner --conductor --verifier-mode production with
#       repair OFF. Its `raw_answer` per case is the A/B input; it cannot be
#       rebuilt (a re-run is a different sample — 11 of 66 raw answers
#       reproduced between two runs of this pinned config).
#   replay_release-2_*        arm A: those exact answers replayed through
#       verify with ONE shared judge pass and ONE repair call, scored three
#       ways (repair-off / repair-on / repair-on with _carry_cleared
#       disabled). `_repair-calls.jsonl` is every replayed model call.
#   replay_release-2b_*       arm A again, second sample. Same command, same
#       inputs, same code: it exists because the two disagree, and the
#       disagreement is the finding.
#   release_repair-fresh.jsonl  arm C: a genuinely fresh --release run with
#       --verifier-repair. It carries GENERATION variance and is never
#       compared claim-for-claim with release-2.
#   audit_repair_release-2*.json  the audit reports over arms A and A'.
#
#     venv/bin/python scripts/audit_repair.py replay \
#       --release data/eval/release_release-2.jsonl \
#       --out-prefix data/eval/replay_release-2 --repair-samples 2
#     venv/bin/python scripts/audit_repair.py \
#       --off data/eval/replay_release-2_repair-off.jsonl \
#       --on  data/eval/replay_release-2_repair-on-carryoff.jsonl \
#       --carry-on data/eval/replay_release-2_repair-on.jsonl \
#       --json data/eval/audit_repair_release-2.json
